---
import PlaygroundShell from '../../components/PlaygroundShell.astro';
import RangeField from '../../components/RangeField.astro';
---
<PlaygroundShell title="Back-of-the-envelope numbers" hint="How many map and reduce tasks a job has, how much work the master tracks, how long a run takes when every worker is busy, and what a failure or a straggler adds. A teaching model with round numbers, not Google's data.">
  <div class="pg est" data-est>
    <div class="pg-row">
      <RangeField label="Input size (TB)" name="d" min="1" max="1000" step="1" value="100" />
      <RangeField label="Input split size (MB)" name="s" min="16" max="64" step="16" value="64" />
      <RangeField label="Reduce tasks (R)" name="r" min="100" max="10000" step="100" value="5000" />
      <RangeField label="Worker machines" name="w" min="100" max="5000" step="100" value="2000" />
      <RangeField label="Input one worker reads and maps (MB per second)" name="p" min="5" max="100" step="5" value="25" />
      <RangeField label="Share of the input kept after the combiner (%)" name="k" min="1" max="100" step="1" value="20" />
      <RangeField label="Workers that die during the run" name="f" min="0" max="300" step="10" value="100" />
      <RangeField label="Run time of the slowest task alone (seconds)" name="t" min="10" max="300" step="10" value="60" />
    </div>
    <dl class="est-out" data-out aria-live="polite"></dl>
    <p class="pg-note">Teaching model, not Google's system or data. From the MapReduce paper: map splits are about 16 to 64 MB, the paper often used M = 200,000 and R = 5,000 with 2,000 workers, the master makes O(M + R) scheduling decisions and keeps O(M x R) state, and its sort took 44% longer without backup tasks. The input size, the speed of a worker, the combiner saving and the number of failures are my guesses that you can change. Assumptions: one terabyte is 1,000,000 MB, tasks are the same size and are spread evenly over the workers, input is read locally, the map phase is the only phase timed, and each dead worker loses on average the map work that fits in its share of the run.</p>
  </div>
</PlaygroundShell>
<script>
  import { query, numberValue, formatOneDecimalBelow100 } from '../../playground';
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-est]').forEach((root) => {
    const q = query(root);
    const val = numberValue(q);
    const OUT_ROWS = 10; // lines in the result list
    const rows = Array.from({ length: OUT_ROWS }, () => { const dt = document.createElement('dt'); const dd = document.createElement('dd'); q('[data-out]').append(dt, dd); return { dt, dd }; });
    const f1 = formatOneDecimalBelow100;
    const pct = (n: number) => `${(Math.round(n * 1000) / 1000).toLocaleString('en-US')}%`;
    function draw() {
      ['d', 's', 'r', 'w', 'p', 'k', 'f', 't'].forEach((key) => (q(`[data-${key}val]`).textContent = String(val(`[data-${key}]`))));
      const tb = val('[data-d]'); const split = val('[data-s]'); const R = val('[data-r]'); const workers = val('[data-w]');
      const speed = val('[data-p]'); const keep = val('[data-k]') / 100; const dead = val('[data-f]'); const tail = val('[data-t]');
      const M = (tb * 1_000_000) / split;
      const mapSeconds = (tb * 1_000_000) / (workers * speed); const rerun = (dead / workers) * mapSeconds;
      const data: [string, string][] = [
        ['Map tasks (M)', f1(M)],
        ['Map tasks per worker', f1(M / workers)],
        ['Master state: map and reduce task pairs (M x R, millions)', f1((M * R) / 1e6)],
        ['Master scheduling decisions (M + R)', f1(M + R)],
        ['Time for the map phase when every worker is busy (minutes)', f1(mapSeconds / 60)],
        ['Intermediate data sent over the network (TB)', f1(tb * keep)],
        ['Share of the workers that die', `${f1((100 * dead) / workers)}%`],
        ['Extra time to redo their finished map work (minutes)', f1(rerun / 60)],
        ['Time lost to the slowest task without a backup, at the end (seconds)', f1(tail)],
        ['Input read over the network if no task runs next to its data (TB)', f1(tb)],
      ];
      rows.forEach((r, i) => { r.dt.textContent = data[i][0]; r.dd.textContent = data[i][1]; });
    }
    root.addEventListener('input', draw);
    draw();
  });
</script>
